{
  "id": "Compound::DB01184 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Domperidone is known to bind to Cyp2b6 and Cyp2c8, which are both members of the cytochrome P450 (CYP) family. These enzymes are critical for the metabolism of xenobiotics, including drugs, and are involved in the oxidation of various substrates. The evidence suggests that Domperidone exerts its effect by inhibiting CYP enzymes, which in turn affects the metabolism of substrates. The observed pathway links Domperidone to Cyp3a4 through a series of molecular interactions, where the inhibition of Cyp2b6 and Cyp2c8 leads to the participation of Cyp3a4 in the metabolic processes of xenobiotics. This mechanism is supported by the presence of a shared pathway where the oxidoreductase activity of Cyp2b6 and Cyp2c8 is linked to Cyp3a4, indicating a potential for cross-talk or functional overlap among these enzymes. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of CYP enzymes in drug metabolism and their potential involvement in the pharmacological effects of Domperidone.",
